    tags: ['economics', 'banking', 'women', 'richmond', 'mutual-aid'],
    summary: `Maggie Lena Walker built financial power from the ground up in Richmond, Virginia. Born just after the Civil War, raised by a mother who took in laundry, Walker became a teacher, organizer, newspaper publisher, fraternal leader, banker, suffragist, and one of the most important Black businesswomen in American history. In 1903, she opened St. Luke Penny Savings Bank and became the first Black woman in the United States to charter and lead a bank. That fact is impressive. The deeper story is why the bank mattered. Walker understood that Black communities needed institutions that could collect pennies, make loans, publish news, train young people, employ women, and keep money circulating where white banks and businesses often refused respect.`,
